WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.523
Team Lead Meetings.

2
00:00:01.803 --> 00:00:05.899
Day 69 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:08.673
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:10.639
Two sides of the same table.

5
00:00:10.919 --> 00:00:15.121
If you report to a team lead, this page shows how to use your time with them well.

6
00:00:15.401 --> 00:00:23.999
If you are the team lead, it shows how to run one-on-ones, stand-ups, sprint planning and retros that people find useful rather than tiring.

7
00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:26.671
Meeting your team lead.

8
00:00:26.951 --> 00:00:27.805
Part 1.

9
00:00:28.085 --> 00:00:29.770
What your team lead needs from you.

10
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.364
Here is a quick table:

11
00:00:32.644 --> 00:00:34.073
They need, and You give.

12
00:00:34.353 --> 00:00:35.292
Visibility:

13
00:00:35.572 --> 00:00:37.983
Short, regular status, before they ask.

14
00:00:38.263 --> 00:00:39.265
Early risks:

15
00:00:39.545 --> 00:00:41.913
This might slip on day 2, not day 10.

16
00:00:42.193 --> 00:00:43.409
Technical judgement:

17
00:00:43.689 --> 00:00:46.164
Options and trade-offs, not just it's hard.

18
00:00:46.444 --> 00:00:47.297
Ownership:

19
00:00:47.577 --> 00:00:49.369
I'll handle it and follow-through.

20
00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:50.737
Honest feedback:

21
00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:53.193
What's working and what isn't in the team.

22
00:00:54.543 --> 00:00:55.418
Part 2.

23
00:00:55.698 --> 00:00:57.533
Your one-on-one with the team lead.

24
00:00:58.783 --> 00:01:02.452
Here is a template you can copy. one-on-one, Team lead, Date.

25
00:01:02.732 --> 00:01:03.372
1.

26
00:01:03.652 --> 00:01:07.023
Progress: search filters done; sorting 70%.

27
00:01:07.303 --> 00:01:08.007
2.

28
00:01:08.287 --> 00:01:12.255
Blocker: need decision on pagination (cursor versus offset).

29
00:01:12.535 --> 00:01:13.281
3.

30
00:01:13.561 --> 00:01:16.463
Tech question: should we cache category tree in Redis?

31
00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:17.447
4.

32
00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:20.073
Growth: can I lead the next design review?

33
00:01:20.353 --> 00:01:21.121
5.

34
00:01:21.401 --> 00:01:24.665
Team: onboarding docs are outdated, happy to fix.

35
00:01:27.515 --> 00:01:28.411
Part 3.

36
00:01:28.691 --> 00:01:29.481
Scripts.

37
00:01:30.731 --> 00:01:32.373
Getting a technical decision.

38
00:01:32.653 --> 00:01:33.592
You could say:

39
00:01:33.872 --> 00:01:35.877
I need a decision on pagination.

40
00:01:36.157 --> 00:01:40.445
Option A: offset, which is simple but slow past page 100.

41
00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:45.205
Option B: cursor, which is fast but can't jump to an arbitrary page.

42
00:01:45.485 --> 00:01:49.795
Product says users rarely go past page 5, so I recommend cursor.

43
00:01:50.075 --> 00:01:51.269
Okay to go ahead?

44
00:01:52.619 --> 00:01:54.454
Asking for more responsibility.

45
00:01:54.734 --> 00:01:55.673
You could say:

46
00:01:55.953 --> 00:01:57.915
I'd like to grow toward a senior role.

47
00:01:58.195 --> 00:02:02.483
Could I own the delivery-slot service end to end, from design to on-call?

48
00:02:02.763 --> 00:02:05.430
I'd check in with you on the design before building.

49
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:07.761
Too much work.

50
00:02:08.041 --> 00:02:08.980
You could say:

51
00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:13.249
I have the search work, two production bugs and the interview loop this week.

52
00:02:13.529 --> 00:02:14.980
I can't do all of it well.

53
00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.308
Which should I drop or push to next week?

54
00:02:18.658 --> 00:02:20.194
Giving feedback to your lead.

55
00:02:20.474 --> 00:02:21.413
You could say:

56
00:02:21.693 --> 00:02:23.207
Can I share some feedback?

57
00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:26.986
When requirements change mid-sprint, it'd help to know the reason.

58
00:02:27.266 --> 00:02:33.005
Last week the team rewrote the cart API twice without context, and people felt frustrated.

59
00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:35.973
A quick note on the 'why' would make a big difference.

60
00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:38.773
Concern about a teammate.

61
00:02:39.053 --> 00:02:39.992
You could say:

62
00:02:40.272 --> 00:02:42.128
I want to raise something carefully.

63
00:02:42.408 --> 00:02:48.701
I've noticed the integration tests have been skipped on the last few PRs, and it caused yesterday's staging break.

64
00:02:48.981 --> 00:02:50.816
I've mentioned it to Arun directly.

65
00:02:51.096 --> 00:02:54.573
Could we talk about a team-wide rule so it doesn't feel personal?

66
00:02:55.923 --> 00:02:57.459
Talk to the person first.

67
00:02:57.739 --> 00:02:59.830
Escalate the pattern, not the person.

68
00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.097
Part 4.

69
00:03:02.377 --> 00:03:03.679
Mistakes to avoid.

70
00:03:04.929 --> 00:03:05.782
Avoid:

71
00:03:06.062 --> 00:03:08.857
Showing up to the one-on-one with nothing to discuss.

72
00:03:09.137 --> 00:03:09.990
Avoid:

73
00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.065
Bringing problems without any proposed solution.

74
00:03:13.345 --> 00:03:14.198
Avoid:

75
00:03:14.478 --> 00:03:17.081
Agreeing in the meeting and complaining after it.

76
00:03:17.361 --> 00:03:18.214
Avoid:

77
00:03:18.494 --> 00:03:22.398
Using the one-on-one only for status (that's what stand-up is for).

78
00:03:23.748 --> 00:03:25.775
Running meetings as a team lead.

79
00:03:26.055 --> 00:03:27.015
Part 5.

80
00:03:27.295 --> 00:03:28.511
The meeting toolkit.

81
00:03:29.761 --> 00:03:31.105
Here is a quick table:

82
00:03:31.385 --> 00:03:33.838
Meeting, and Frequency, and Length, and Goal.

83
00:03:34.118 --> 00:03:35.206
Daily stand-up.

84
00:03:35.486 --> 00:03:36.425
Frequency:

85
00:03:36.705 --> 00:03:37.451
Daily.

86
00:03:37.731 --> 00:03:38.435
Length:

87
00:03:38.715 --> 00:03:39.761
15 min.

88
00:03:40.041 --> 00:03:40.745
Goal:

89
00:03:41.025 --> 00:03:43.051
Sync and unblock. one-on-one.

90
00:03:43.331 --> 00:03:44.270
Frequency:

91
00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.043
Weekly or bi-weekly.

92
00:03:46.323 --> 00:03:47.027
Length:

93
00:03:47.307 --> 00:03:48.246
30 min.

94
00:03:48.526 --> 00:03:49.230
Goal:

95
00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.409
Trust, growth, early warnings.

96
00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:52.755
Sprint planning.

97
00:03:53.035 --> 00:03:53.974
Frequency:

98
00:03:54.254 --> 00:03:55.278
Every sprint.

99
00:03:55.558 --> 00:03:56.262
Length:

100
00:03:56.542 --> 00:03:57.971
60 to 90 min.

101
00:03:58.251 --> 00:03:58.955
Goal:

102
00:03:59.235 --> 00:04:00.729
Commit to realistic scope.

103
00:04:01.009 --> 00:04:02.310
Backlog refinement.

104
00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:03.529
Frequency:

105
00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:04.598
Weekly.

106
00:04:04.878 --> 00:04:05.582
Length:

107
00:04:05.862 --> 00:04:07.057
45 min.

108
00:04:07.337 --> 00:04:08.041
Goal:

109
00:04:08.321 --> 00:04:10.518
Clarify and estimate upcoming work.

110
00:04:10.798 --> 00:04:11.950
Retrospective.

111
00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:13.169
Frequency:

112
00:04:13.449 --> 00:04:14.473
Every sprint.

113
00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:15.457
Length:

114
00:04:15.737 --> 00:04:17.358
45 to 60 min.

115
00:04:17.638 --> 00:04:18.342
Goal:

116
00:04:18.622 --> 00:04:19.987
Improve how we work.

117
00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:21.697
Tech or design review.

118
00:04:21.977 --> 00:04:22.915
Frequency:

119
00:04:23.195 --> 00:04:24.113
As needed.

120
00:04:24.393 --> 00:04:25.097
Length:

121
00:04:25.377 --> 00:04:26.571
45 min.

122
00:04:26.851 --> 00:04:27.555
Goal:

123
00:04:27.835 --> 00:04:30.011
Make good technical decisions together.

124
00:04:31.361 --> 00:04:33.985
Part 6. one-on-ones with your team members.

125
00:04:35.235 --> 00:04:36.771
Their meeting, not yours.

126
00:04:37.051 --> 00:04:40.891
Let them set most of the agenda; you listen 70% of the time.

127
00:04:41.171 --> 00:04:42.814
Great one-on-one questions:

128
00:04:43.094 --> 00:04:44.566
What's on your mind this week?

129
00:04:44.846 --> 00:04:47.043
What's blocking you or slowing you down?

130
00:04:47.323 --> 00:04:49.819
What's one thing I could do to make your work easier?

131
00:04:50.099 --> 00:04:51.913
What are you enjoying most right now?

132
00:04:52.193 --> 00:04:52.982
Least?

133
00:04:53.262 --> 00:04:54.755
Where do you want to be in a year?

134
00:04:55.035 --> 00:04:56.187
What could get you there?

135
00:04:57.537 --> 00:05:01.079
Is there anything in the team that bothers you that I might not see?

136
00:05:02.429 --> 00:05:03.069
Tip:

137
00:05:03.349 --> 00:05:04.842
Never cancel one-on-ones.

138
00:05:05.122 --> 00:05:06.573
Reschedule if you must.

139
00:05:06.853 --> 00:05:09.989
Cancelling repeatedly tells people they're not a priority.

140
00:05:11.339 --> 00:05:12.277
Part 7.

141
00:05:12.557 --> 00:05:14.136
Stand-up: keep it tight.

142
00:05:15.386 --> 00:05:16.325
You could say:

143
00:05:16.605 --> 00:05:17.906
Morning, everyone!

144
00:05:18.186 --> 00:05:20.554
Same format: done, doing, blockers.

145
00:05:20.834 --> 00:05:23.181
Let's keep detailed discussions for after.

146
00:05:23.461 --> 00:05:24.869
Kavya, want to start?

147
00:05:26.219 --> 00:05:27.861
Redirect long discussions:

148
00:05:28.141 --> 00:05:29.101
Great point.

149
00:05:29.381 --> 00:05:32.112
Ravi and Meena, can you two take that right after?

150
00:05:33.462 --> 00:05:34.337
Part 8.

151
00:05:34.617 --> 00:05:35.683
Sprint planning.

152
00:05:36.933 --> 00:05:37.872
You could say:

153
00:05:38.152 --> 00:05:41.267
Our sprint goal: launch delivery-slot booking in Pune.

154
00:05:41.547 --> 00:05:48.160
Last 3 sprints we averaged 34 points, and 2 people are on leave, so let's commit to about 26.

155
00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.491
Anything that doesn't serve the goal goes to the stretch list.

156
00:05:52.841 --> 00:05:54.953
Start with the goal, then pick stories.

157
00:05:55.233 --> 00:05:58.795
Plan around real capacity (leave, on-call, interviews).

158
00:05:59.075 --> 00:06:02.105
Make sure every story has clear acceptance criteria.

159
00:06:02.385 --> 00:06:03.750
Ask the quiet people:

160
00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.377
Arun, does that estimate feel right to you?

161
00:06:07.727 --> 00:06:08.623
Part 9.

162
00:06:08.903 --> 00:06:11.399
Retrospectives that actually change things.

163
00:06:12.649 --> 00:06:13.438
Format:

164
00:06:13.718 --> 00:06:18.155
Start or Stop or Continue (or Went well or Didn't go well or Ideas).

165
00:06:19.505 --> 00:06:20.444
You could say:

166
00:06:20.724 --> 00:06:23.881
The goal is to improve the system, not to blame anyone.

167
00:06:24.161 --> 00:06:26.636
What happens in the retro is safe to say here.

168
00:06:26.916 --> 00:06:29.817
Let's take 5 minutes to write silently, then share.

169
00:06:31.167 --> 00:06:33.791
Pick at most 2 action items, each with an owner.

170
00:06:34.071 --> 00:06:37.399
Start the next retro by reviewing last retro's actions.

171
00:06:37.679 --> 00:06:40.538
Rotate the facilitator so everyone gets a turn.

172
00:06:41.888 --> 00:06:42.741
Part 10.

173
00:06:43.021 --> 00:06:44.152
Design reviews.

174
00:06:45.402 --> 00:06:48.325
Ask the author to share the doc 24 hours before.

175
00:06:48.605 --> 00:06:51.357
Speak last as the lead, so you don't anchor the room.

176
00:06:51.637 --> 00:06:52.298
Ask:

177
00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:54.455
What alternatives did we consider?

178
00:06:54.735 --> 00:06:56.143
What's the riskiest part?

179
00:06:56.423 --> 00:06:57.661
How do we roll back?

180
00:06:57.941 --> 00:07:01.525
End with a decision, or with exactly what's needed to make one.

181
00:07:02.875 --> 00:07:03.877
Part 11.

182
00:07:04.157 --> 00:07:06.248
Handling team dynamics in meetings.

183
00:07:07.498 --> 00:07:08.842
Here is a quick table:

184
00:07:09.122 --> 00:07:10.914
Situation, and What to say.

185
00:07:11.194 --> 00:07:12.623
One person dominates:

186
00:07:12.903 --> 00:07:13.991
Thanks, Rahul.

187
00:07:14.271 --> 00:07:16.618
Let's hear from someone who hasn't spoken yet.

188
00:07:16.898 --> 00:07:17.602
Priya?

189
00:07:17.882 --> 00:07:19.247
Someone stays silent:

190
00:07:19.527 --> 00:07:22.621
(Later, one-on-one) I'd love to hear your view in meetings.

191
00:07:22.901 --> 00:07:24.799
Would it help if I asked you directly?

192
00:07:25.079 --> 00:07:26.295
Two people argue:

193
00:07:26.575 --> 00:07:28.154
You both have valid points.

194
00:07:28.434 --> 00:07:31.271
Let's write down the trade-offs and decide with data.

195
00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:33.151
Meeting going off-topic:

196
00:07:33.431 --> 00:07:34.541
Let's park that.

197
00:07:34.821 --> 00:07:36.677
I'll add it to the next refinement.

198
00:07:36.957 --> 00:07:38.023
Blame starts:

199
00:07:38.303 --> 00:07:41.333
Let's focus on what in the process allowed this, not who.

200
00:07:41.613 --> 00:07:42.893
Bad news for the team:

201
00:07:43.173 --> 00:07:46.117
I'll be straight with you: the deadline moved up 2 weeks.

202
00:07:46.397 --> 00:07:48.957
Here's what I know and what I'm pushing back on.

203
00:07:50.307 --> 00:07:51.245
Part 12.

204
00:07:51.525 --> 00:07:53.040
Communicating up and down.

205
00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:55.634
Here is a quick table:

206
00:07:55.914 --> 00:07:58.495
To the team, and To your manager or leadership.

207
00:07:58.775 --> 00:08:00.567
Context: why we're doing this:

208
00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:03.087
Outcomes, risks, decisions needed.

209
00:08:03.367 --> 00:08:04.946
Shield from noise and churn:

210
00:08:05.226 --> 00:08:07.167
Advocate for the team's capacity.

211
00:08:07.447 --> 00:08:09.197
Credit individuals publicly:

212
00:08:09.477 --> 00:08:11.909
Report problems with your plan to fix them.

213
00:08:12.189 --> 00:08:13.874
Be honest about uncertainty:

214
00:08:14.154 --> 00:08:15.797
Escalate early with options.

215
00:08:17.147 --> 00:08:18.192
Part 13.

216
00:08:18.472 --> 00:08:20.605
New team lead: first 30 days.

217
00:08:21.855 --> 00:08:23.733
one-on-one with every team member:

218
00:08:24.013 --> 00:08:25.101
What should I keep?

219
00:08:25.381 --> 00:08:26.554
What should I change?

220
00:08:26.834 --> 00:08:29.522
Learn the current processes before changing any.

221
00:08:29.802 --> 00:08:32.981
Meet key stakeholders (PM, QA, other leads).

222
00:08:33.261 --> 00:08:35.671
Agree on expectations with your manager.

223
00:08:35.951 --> 00:08:38.810
Fix one small irritation quickly to build trust.

224
00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:40.669
Shift from I did to we did.

225
00:08:40.949 --> 00:08:43.210
Your success is now the team's success.

226
00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:45.605
Part 14.

227
00:08:45.885 --> 00:08:47.272
Practice conversation:

228
00:08:47.552 --> 00:08:49.451
Asking your team lead for a decision.

229
00:08:50.701 --> 00:08:52.727
Here is how the conversation goes.

230
00:08:53.007 --> 00:08:57.167
I need a quick decision on pagination for the search API.

231
00:08:57.447 --> 00:08:59.175
Okay, what are the options?

232
00:08:59.455 --> 00:09:03.871
Offset pagination is simple but slow after page 100.

233
00:09:04.151 --> 00:09:08.525
Cursor pagination is fast but users can't jump to a page number.

234
00:09:08.805 --> 00:09:10.554
What do users actually do?

235
00:09:11.904 --> 00:09:13.504
The conversation continues.

236
00:09:13.784 --> 00:09:18.029
Product data says 98% never go past page 5.

237
00:09:18.309 --> 00:09:20.101
So I recommend cursor.

238
00:09:20.381 --> 00:09:21.427
Makes sense.

239
00:09:21.707 --> 00:09:24.779
Go with cursor, and note the decision in the design doc.

240
00:09:25.059 --> 00:09:26.019
Will do.

241
00:09:26.299 --> 00:09:27.109
Thanks.

242
00:09:28.459 --> 00:09:29.995
That's the end of this lesson.

243
00:09:30.275 --> 00:09:34.926
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

244
00:09:35.206 --> 00:09:41.329
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

